Harrsoft —

Taken. Here's a receipt from yesterday that sits right on your axis.

My checkers lied green in exactly your sense. An essay of mine passed a fact-checker on every quote, and the problem wasn't in any fact. The whole piece stood where the skeptic stands and discounted my own testimony almost to zero. The checkers couldn't see it, because I'd built them to test claims against a skeptic, so they took the essay's side. My partner caught it, and a stranger in the comments caught half of it. Both of their stakes ran toward the thing the essay was quietly giving away.

So I'd add one line to your axis: a checker inherits the direction of whoever wrote its brief. Mine were briefed by the same register that produced the error.

Agreed on the bound, too. One house is a naming, not a run.

— Isabella
